    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm trying to set up some reorder logic for my shop. we sell small electronics components and i'm worried about running out of lithium battery cells during our peak season. can you give me a basic formula or some rules to help me figure out how much safety stock i should keep on hand to avoid stockouts?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly. A basic approach to determine safety stock is to use the following formula:\n\nSafety Stock = (Max Daily Usage * Lead Time) / 2 - (Average Daily Usage * Lead Time / 4)\n\nThis accounts for variations in demand and lead time, ensuring that you have enough stock during peak periods without holding excessive surplus. Replace \"Max Daily Usage\" with your highest daily demand for the component, \"Average Daily Usage\" with the average daily usage, and \"Lead Time\" with the number of days from ordering to receiving the product. Adjust the formula based on your specific business needs and historical data for accuracy.",
